{"step_index":86,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-16T08:32:11Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-16T12:32:11+04:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-16T12:32:11+04:00\nFile Path: `file:///home/koby/infra-management-system/roles/vsphere_vm/defaults/main.yml`\nTotal Lines: 69\nTotal Bytes: 3975\nShowing lines 1 to 69\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: ---\n2: # =============================================================================\n3: # Rôle vsphere_vm — création de VM par clone de template + guest customization.\n4: # Calqué sur roles/pki_cert : piloté par API (ici vCenter), idempotent (vmware_guest\n5: # ne recrée pas une VM existante), exécuté côté contrôleur (le poste), secrets en vault.\n6: # Tout est paramétrable ; le rôle ASSERTE les valeurs critiques dans tasks/main.yml.\n7: # =============================================================================\n8: \n9: # --- Spec de la VM à créer ---------------------------------------------------\n10: # Fournie par le playbook via `vsphere_vm_spec` (un élément de la table wikic-vms.yml).\n11: # Schéma attendu :\n12: #   name:      hostname court (= nom de la VM dans vCenter)         [requis]\n13: #   template:  \"base\" | \"docker\" (clé vers vsphere_vm_templates)    [requis]\n14: #   vcpu:      nombre de vCPU                                        [requis]\n15: #   ram_mb:    RAM en Mo                                             [requis]\n16: #   os_gb:     taille du disque OS en Go                            [requis]\n17: #   data_gb:   taille du disque data en Go (optionnel, 0/absent = pas de disque data)\n18: #   data_datastore: datastore du disque data (optionnel, défaut vsphere_vm_datastore_data)\n19: #   portgroup: nom du port-group / VLAN distribué                   [requis]\n20: #   ip:        IPv4 statique                                        [requis]\n21: #   netmask:   masque (ex. 255.255.255.240)                         [requis]\n22: #   gateway:   passerelle                                           [requis]\n23: vsphere_vm_spec: {}\n24: \n25: # Mapping clé logique -> nom réel du template dans vCenter.\n26: # `debian13-base` existe déjà ; `debian13-docker` est à créer depuis th2-ansid-run1\n27: # (cf. docs/provisionnement-vsphere-wikic.md). Les templates Linux DOIVENT embarquer\n28: # open-vm-tools + perl pour que la guest customization s'applique.\n29: vsphere_vm_templates:\n30:   base: \"debian13-base\"\n31:   docker: \"debian13-docker\"\n32: \n33: # --- Connexion vCenter (SECRET — fournir vcenter_* via un group_vars chiffré) -------\n34: # cf. inventories/corp/group_vars/all_vault.yml (Vault Password du repo).\n35: vsphere_vm_hostname: \"{{ vcenter_hostname | default('') }}\"\n36: vsphere_vm_username: \"{{ vcenter_username | default('') }}\"\n37: vsphere_vm_password: \"{{ vcenter_password | default('') }}\"\n38: # vCenter présente une CA interne ; passer à true si la CA est dans le magasin du poste.\n39: vsphere_vm_validate_certs: false\n40: \n41: # --- Placement dans l'inventaire vCenter (À CONFIRMER avec l'équipe virtu) ----------\n42: vsphere_vm_datacenter: \"TH2 - Téléware House\"\n43: vsphere_vm_cluster: \"\"           # nom du cluster vSphere (ex. \"Cluster-TH2\")  [requis]\n44: vsphere_vm_resource_pool: \"\"     # resource pool (vide = pool racine du cluster)\n45: vsphere_vm_folder: \"\"            # dossier VM cible (ex. \"/TH2.../vm/WIKI\")     [requis]\n46: \n47: # Datastores : OS (tous) + data performant (SSD/SAS) pour pg1 / nfs2.  [requis]\n48: vsphere_vm_datastore: \"\"         # datastore du disque OS\n49: vsphere_vm_datastore_data: \"{{ vsphere_vm_datastore }}\"  # datastore disque data (surchargeable)\n50: \n51: # --- Identité réseau invité (guest customization) ----------------------------------\n52: vsphere_vm_domain: \"corp.intranet.local\"\n53: # Résolveurs DNS de la zone CORP.  [requis — au moins un]\n54: vsphere_vm_dns_servers: []\n55: # Suffixes de recherche DNS appliqués dans l'invité.\n56: vsphere_vm_dns_suffixes:\n57:   - \"{{ vsphere_vm_domain }}\"\n58: \n59: # --- Comportement ------------------------------------------------------------------\n60: # Power on à la création (la customization Linux s'applique au 1er boot).\n61: vsphere_vm_power_on: true\n62: # Attendre que VMware tools remontent l'IP avant de rendre la main.\n63: vsphere_vm_wait_for_ip: true\n64: vsphere_vm_wait_timeout: 600\n65: # Firmware du guest (la fiche indique « à confirmer » — efi par défaut Debian 13).\n66: vsphere_vm_firmware: efi\n67: vsphere_vm_scsi: paravirtual\n68: vsphere_vm_guest_id: debian12_64Guest   # Debian 13 : pas encore d'id dédié, debian12_64Guest convient\n69: \nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
{"step_index":87,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-16T08:32:11Z","content":"The playbook [`playbooks/provision-vsphere-vms.yml`](file:///home/koby/infra-management-system/playbooks/provision-vsphere-vms.yml) is an **infrastructure-as-code provisioning script** that automates the creation of virtual machines directly on your vCenter / vSphere cluster.\n\nHere is an exact breakdown of what it does, how it works, and what it does **not** touch:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Where and How It Runs\n\n* **Runs locally on your workstation (`localhost`, `connection: local`)**:\n  It does **not** run on a Semaphore runner. Only your local workstation has network reachability to port `443` of the internal vCenter.\n* **Driven by the [`roles/vsphere_vm`](file:///home/koby/infra-management-system/roles/vsphere_vm/tasks/main.yml) role**:\n  The playbook loops over VM specifications defined in [`playbooks/vars/wikic-vms.yml`](file:///home/koby/infra-management-system/playbooks/vars/wikic-vms.yml).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Step-by-Step Execution Flow\n\nWhen executed, for each target VM, Ansible performs:\n\n#### Step 1: Pre-flight Validations (Safety Asserts)\nBefore making any calls to vCenter, it asserts that:\n* vCenter credentials (`vcenter_hostname`, `vcenter_username`, `vcenter_password`) are provided (via your Ansible vault).\n* Placement settings are defined: Datacenter (`TH2 - Téléware House`), Cluster, Target VM Folder, Datastore, and DNS resolvers.\n* The VM specification has valid CPU, RAM, OS disk size, static IP, netmask, gateway, and VLAN portgroup.\n\n#### Step 2: Disk Layout Calculation\n* Dynamically constructs the disk layout with **thin provisioning**.\n* Configures the OS disk on the main datastore.\n* If `data_gb > 0` (e.g. 100 GB for database data), it attaches a secondary disk to the fast/SSD datastore.\n\n#### Step 3: vCenter Cloning & Hardware Sizing (`vmware_guest`)\nAnsible contacts the vCenter API over HTTPS and:\n1. **Checks if the VM already exists**:\n   * **If it already exists**: it leaves it untouched (**idempotent**).\n   * **If it does not exist**: it clones the base template (e.g. `debian13-base`).\n2. **Applies hardware specs**:\n   * Slices vCPU and RAM.\n   * Configures SCSI controller to VMware Paravirtual (`pvscsi`).\n   * Sets boot firmware to `efi` and guest OS ID to `debian12_64Guest`.\n   * Connects the vNIC to the specified distributed portgroup / VLAN.\n\n#### Step 4: VMware Guest Customization (OS Network Identity)\n* Injects OS configuration without booting an ISO:\n  * Hostname, Domain (`corp.intranet.local`), and DNS search domains.\n  * Static IPv4, netmask, and default gateway.\n* *(Prerequisite: requires `open-vm-tools` and `perl` installed in the base template so VMware Tools can configure `/etc/network/interfaces` upon boot).*\n\n#### Step 5: Power On & Health Check\n* Powers on the VM.\n* Waits (up to 10 minutes) until `open-vm-tools` boots and reports the guest's active IP address back to vCenter.\n* Prints a summary of the provisioned VM.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What VMs Are Defined in the Specs?\n\nCurrently, [`playbooks/vars/wikic-vms.yml`](file:///home/koby/infra-management-system/playbooks/vars/wikic-vms.yml) defines 3 machines for the WIKI CORP cluster:\n1. **`trp-wikic-pg1`**: PostgreSQL database (2 vCPU, 4 GB RAM, 20 GB OS, 100 GB Data SSD on VLAN `Bus_WebDBM_C`, IP `172.30.250.107`).\n2. **`trp-wintc-rdb1`**: Redis cache (1 vCPU, 2 GB RAM, 20 GB OS on VLAN `Bus_WebRDB_C`, IP `172.30.250.145`).\n3. **`trp-wintc-be2`**: Outline Docker backend testbed (2 vCPU, 4 GB RAM, 40 GB OS, 30 GB Data on VLAN `Bus_WebBE_C`, IP `172.30.250.130`).\n\n> **Targeted execution**: You do not have to create all 3 at once. The playbook supports `-e only=<hostname>` (e.g. `-e only=trp-wikic-pg1`) to act on a single VM.\n\n---\n\n### 4. What This Playbook Does NOT Do\n\n* **No application installation**: It does not install PostgreSQL, Redis, or Docker inside the VM. It purely creates and boots the bare virtual machine with OS and network ready.\n* **No destructive actions**: It never deletes, resets, or formats existing VMs.","thinking":"The execution occurs on the local workstation, indicated by the `localhost` connection. This is necessary because the local machine has direct network access to the vCenter management interface on port 443. This is unlike Semaphore runners, which are firewall-restricted.\n\n\nThe agent directly interfaces with vCenter, obtaining VM specifications from a YAML file detailing resources like vCPU, RAM, and storage, along with network configurations. It uses credentials and settings, likely stored in a vault for security, alongside cluster, folder, datastore, and DNS server configurations. The playbook utilizes a command-line variable to limit operations to a specific VM. Initial execution involves comprehensive pre-flight validations, ensuring connection details and infrastructure settings are present and valid.\n\n\nThe agent constructs the virtual machine's disk layout, with an OS disk and an optional data disk based on the input specifications. It then interacts with vCenter, checking for existing VMs with the specified name; existing VMs are simply ensured to be present while new VMs trigger a clone operation from a template, including hardware and network configurations. It powers on the VM, waits for the guest tools to signal a usable IP, and reports the outcome.\n\n\n"}
